@@ -0,0 +1,202 @@
+# TypeScript pilot
+
+This pilot connects TypeScript exercises to Mathua's existing generator interface.
+It uses the existing engine, HTTP routes, SQLite store, prerequisite rules, mastery model and report workflow.
+It serves Mathua's existing study interface beside the API.
+Exercises, lessons and prerequisite edges come from an editorial package outside this patch.
+
+The TypeScript content and grader are a separate adaptation.
+They are not Math Academy content or an official Mathua curriculum.
+
+## Scope
+
+The pilot reads `curriculo.json` and `exercicios.json` from the package `conteudo/typescript-v1`
+of the companion `anki-typescript` repository. It finds that directory above the working directory,
+or takes it from `-content`. Nothing is copied into this patch, so the package stays the one source.
+
+`integratedThroughStage` in `content.go` is the last ladder stage served. It is 5 now:
+23 concepts, 25 families and 150 cases of stages D01 to D05. Later stages have no grader yet.
+
+| What the package holds | How the existing engine receives it |
+| --- | --- |
+| Concept, with prerequisites | One concept of the DAG, with the same ID and edges |
+| Family, a task with one criterion | One section of the concept's lesson (a knowledge point) |
+| Case, one concrete question | One problem of the concept's generator |
+| Stage lesson | The lesson of every concept of that stage, once per section |
+| `choice` family | Question text ending in `A) ...` blocks, which the interface draws as buttons |
+
+The three concepts of the first pilot keep their IDs: `ts.value.numeric_literal` (family F002),
+`ts.type.number` (F005) and `ts.binding.const_number` (F010). Stored progress stays attached to them.
+
+A family is one section because the existing stepper stays on a section after a wrong answer.
+The practice route passes the section as `kp_ordinal`, and the generator serves that family only.
+The route accepts `kp_ordinal` 1 to 3, so the loader refuses a concept with more than three families.
+
+The bank is finite: six cases per family. The generator walks the cases in order, so a batch never
+repeats a case while others remain. With `NEXT_PUBLIC_TS_PILOT=1` the study page excludes the
+questions it showed most recently, which leaves the case shown longest ago.
+The practice route now excludes a multi-line question as a whole. Before, it compared the excluded
+text line by line, so a question with a choice list was never excluded.
+
+At start the pilot grades the model answer of every served case with `grade.cjs`, in one process.
+A family without a grader, or a model answer its own grader rejects, stops the start with the list.
+
+The prerequisite edges are the package's teaching choice.
+They do not define a mandatory sequence for all TypeScript learning.
+The native mastery thresholds remain in `internal/mastery/evidence.go`, and every concept keeps the
+threshold the first pilot used. The pilot does not validate their educational calibration.
+Reports preserve the concept, the question, the expected answer and the learner answer for replay.
+Concept and question identify one family and one case of the package; `content.lookup` returns them.
+The report row does not store the family, the case or the content version as separate fields.
+
+## Install and test
+
+Use the Go version declared in the repository's `go.mod`.
+Use a Node version that satisfies `web/next-app/package.json` and install npm.
+Run these commands from the repository root:
+
+```sh
+npm ci --prefix cmd/typescript-pilot --ignore-scripts
+npm ci --prefix web/next-app
+NEXT_PUBLIC_TS_PILOT=1 NEXT_PUBLIC_SAME_ORIGIN_API=1 NEXT_TELEMETRY_DISABLED=1 npm run build --prefix web/next-app
+go test ./cmd/typescript-pilot -count=1 -v
+go build -o pilot-bin ./cmd/typescript-pilot
+./pilot-bin
+```
+
+Open `http://127.0.0.1:8081` in a browser.
+The root redirects to the first concept in the existing `/learn` page.
+The exported frontend uses the API on the same origin.
+Select `Próximo` to start and use `Praticar novamente` to continue a concept.
+The native section completion card and the engine's mastery state have different criteria.
+Use the next-concept link to continue after the engine marks a concept as mastered.
+
+`NEXT_PUBLIC_TS_PILOT=1` is a build-time switch read by `web/next-app/lib/pilot.ts`.
+Without it, the interface builds exactly as the original Mathua interface.
+With it, the build changes three things for this pilot:
+
+- The answer field does not render the mathematical symbol palette.
+- The study path uses Portuguese text: answer field, grading, continue, practise again, prerequisites and problem reports.
+- Navigation omits the concept graph, the glossary, the resources group and the footer, which show the bundled mathematics catalog.
+
+The learner's own problem reports are on `/my-reports`, which the pilot opens for the guest identity.
+Other pages keep the original interface language.
+
+The command prints its local study and API addresses.
+Use `-addr` to set the listen address.
+Use `-db` to set the SQLite database path.
+Keep the adjacent `.jwt` file with the database to preserve login tokens across process restarts.
+Use `-node` or `-grader` to set non-default executable and script paths.
+Use `-content` to set the editorial package directory.
+The lesson files the engine reads are written to `<db>.lessons` on every start.
+Use `-web` to set the static export directory.
+Run the binary from the repository root with the default paths.
+Without a frontend build, the API still starts and the command prints the build instruction.
+
+## Existing routes used by the test
+
+| Operation | Native route |
+| --- | --- |
+| Create learner identity | `POST /api/auth/guest` |
+| Request exercises | `GET /api/lessons/{concept_id}/practice` |
+| Submit an answer | `POST /api/study/answer` |
+| Report a question | `POST /api/reports` |
+| Read report resolutions | `GET /api/reports/mine` |
+| Replay a reported answer | `POST /api/admin/reports/{id}/grade` |
+| Resolve a report | `PATCH /api/admin/reports/{id}` |
+
+Send the returned bearer token on learner requests.
+Send the served question text and a unique `submission_id` with each logical answer.
+Reuse that ID when retrying the same submission.
+Request the next variant with `count=1` and `exclude` set to the previous question text.
+The test supplies actual JSON requests and checks the persisted state.
+Administrative routes use the existing role checks.
+The test grants an operator role only inside its temporary database.
+
+## Grading contract
+
+Each family of the package has its own criterion. `grade.cjs` holds one grader per code family
+and one per answer mode for `choice`, `token` and `value`. The `value` grader reads a number or a
+boolean written in TypeScript notation; text labels, arrays and `undefined` belong to later stages.
+
+| Family | Accepted | Not accepted |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| F002 numeric literal | `15000`, `15_000` | `"15000"`, `10000 + 5000` |
+| F003 text literal | `"reembolso-01"`, `'reembolso-01'` | a template literal, other content, no quotes |
+| F004 boolean literal | `false`, `true;` | `"false"`, the opposite value, `True` |
+| F010 numeric `const` | no annotation, `: number`, or any type the value fits, such as `: 15000` | `let`, other name or value, text initializer, a type the checker resolves to `any` or `unknown`, `: string` |
+| F011 text `const` | no annotation, `: string` or any type the value fits; either quote | `let`, other name or content, `: any`, `: number` |
+| F016 `name > limit` | `amount > 10000`, `10000 < amount`, parentheses, `10_000` | `>=`, the opposite comparison, another name or limit |
+| F018 `name === "text"` | either operand order, either quote | `=`, `==`, another name or text |
+| F019 `a && b` | either operand order | `\|\|`, `&`, one name only |
+| F020 `!name` | `!isValid`, `!(isValid)` | the name alone, a double negation |
+| F021 const object | properties in any order, quoted keys, parentheses | an array, a missing, extra or wrong property |
+| F022 property access | `refund.amount`, `refund["amount"]` | another property, another object |
+| F023 array | the elements in the requested order, declared with `const` or `let` | another order, another or repeated element |
+| `value` families F017, F024 | `true`, `false`, `15000`, `15_000` | the value in quotes, the expression itself |
+| `token` families | the exact name or symbol; spaces and comments around it are ignored | another case, such as `Number`; two names |
+| `choice` families | the letter of the correct alternative, or its text | any other alternative |
+
+A learner answers a choice by letter. The engine resolves the letter against the served question
+and hands the grader the text of that alternative, as it does for Mathua's own choice questions.
+The reference answer shown after grading is therefore the text of the correct alternative.
+
+A wrong answer shows the explanation of that case. The grader also states what is wrong with the
+answer when the answer itself supports it, for example `let` where the task asks for `const`.
+With `NEXT_PUBLIC_TS_PILOT=1` the study page shows that sentence; the original page drops it.
+
+The constant prompt states that the type annotation is optional.
+Both answers below satisfy that task:
+
+```ts
+const amount: number = 15000;
+const amount = 15000;
+```
+
+Each submission contains only one of these declarations.
+The checker rejects `let`, an incorrect name, an incorrect value, a string initializer or extra statements.
+A reference answer that is not in the served content is reported as not graded, never as wrong.
+It accepts numeric separators, whitespace and ordinary comments.
+It checks TypeScript syntax and types without executing or emitting learner code.
+An answer is typechecked after the code the question shows, its `context_code`.
+The comments `@ts-ignore`, `@ts-expect-error` and `@ts-nocheck` are neutralised before the check,
+so they cannot hide a type error.
+An answer with a triple-slash directive (`/// <reference ... />`) is rejected. The grader asks the
+compiler whether the answer has one, so a comment or a text that only contains those characters is not one.
+A property key may be written with quotes of either kind or with backticks without substitution.
+An answer nested too deeply for the parser is rejected as an answer, not reported as an outage.
+Compiler file reads are restricted to the virtual answer and TypeScript's standard library declarations.
+Compiler failure returns an unavailable result instead of a learner error.
+The added `ungraded` field transmits that distinction on an unavailable result.
+
+### Optional `ungraded` field
+
+Treat `ungraded === true` as "the grader failed, no answer was evaluated".
+For a successful HTTP response, an absent field or `false` means the answer was evaluated.
+Use `correct` to distinguish a correct answer from an incorrect one.
+Handle HTTP failures before interpreting these fields.
+
+With `submission_id`, the engine stores normal answer JSON in `ResponseJSON`.
+That JSON omits `ungraded` and the server returns it before the fallback response map.
+Retries return the same saved body.
+The existing `LearnStepper` already checks `if (res.ungraded)`.
+This pilot preserves that optional-field contract and documents it instead of changing stored JSON.
+
+## Feedback and limits
+
+A report stores the concrete exercise and answer.
+The study page passes the expected answer and explanation to its existing report button after grading.
+A reviewer can replay it with the same grader, record a resolution and return that resolution to the learner.
+Reporting and replay do not write learning evidence.
+The native `repair.Run` operation can correct historical false negatives after a grader change.
+The test checks its preview and apply paths with a simulated historical error.
+That repair does not recalculate XP.
+
+Feedback does not automatically generate new exercise families or tune selection in this pilot.
+A reviewer still decides how to change a prompt or grader.
+The prerequisite test covers practice and study submission without diagnostic placement.
+The original diagnostic placement policy remains available in Mathua.
+The API test restarts the server and reopens SQLite within the test process.
+The companion `anki-typescript` repository supplies `process-smoke.py` for real process restarts
+and `browser-smoke.mjs` for the existing interface against the real API.
+These checks do not measure a deployed installation or long-term retention.
diff --git a/cmd/typescript-pilot/content.go b/cmd/typescript-pilot/content.go
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..2747118e
--- /dev/null
+++ b/cmd/typescript-pilot/content.go
@@ -0,0 +1,422 @@
+package main
+
+import (
+	"encoding/json"
+	"fmt"
+	"os"
+	"path/filepath"
+	"strings"
+	"sync/atomic"
+
+	"github.com/chuma-beep/mathua/internal/concepts"
+	"github.com/chuma-beep/mathua/internal/generator"
+	"github.com/chuma-beep/mathua/internal/grader"
+)
+
+// integratedThroughStage is the last ladder stage whose families have graders in grade.cjs.
+// Raising it serves more of the content package. Startup grades every model answer of the
+// served stages and refuses to run when a grader is missing or rejects its own model answer.
+const integratedThroughStage = 5
+
+// contentDirName is where the editorial package lives in the repository that carries this pilot.
+const contentDirName = "conteudo/typescript-v1"
+
+// maxFamiliesPerConcept follows the existing practice route, which accepts kp_ordinal 1 to 3.
+const maxFamiliesPerConcept = 3
+
+// casesPerFamily is the bank size of the package. The study page rotates a section's bank with
+// this size (PILOT_BANK in web/next-app/lib/pilot.ts), so the loader refuses any other size.
+const casesPerFamily = 6
+
+type choice struct {
+	ID   string `json:"id"`
+	Text string `json:"text"`
+}
+
+type exerciseCase struct {
+	ID                string   `json:"id"`
+	Prompt            string   `json:"prompt"`
+	ContextCode       string   `json:"context_code"`
+	Answer            string   `json:"answer"`
+	AnswerExplanation string   `json:"answer_explanation"`
+	Explanation       string   `json:"explanation"`
+	CodeForm          string   `json:"code_form"`
+	Choices           []choice `json:"choices"`
+}
+
+type family struct {
+	ID        string         `json:"id"`
+	ConceptID string         `json:"concept_id"`
+	Mode      string         `json:"mode"`
+	Cases     []exerciseCase `json:"cases"`
+}
+
+type conceptSpec struct {
+	ID            string   `json:"id"`
+	Label         string   `json:"label"`
+	Stage         int      `json:"stage"`
+	Prerequisites []string `json:"prerequisites"`
+	FamilyIDs     []string `json:"family_ids"`
+}
+
+// content is the served part of the editorial package: the concepts up to one stage.
+type content struct {
+	Version  string
+	Concepts []conceptSpec
+	families map[string]*family
+	lessons  map[int]string
+}
+
+// gradeJob is what grade.cjs needs to judge one answer. The learner's answer is filled in last.
+type gradeJob struct {
+	Case        string `json:"case"`
+	Family      string `json:"family"`
+	Mode        string `json:"mode"`
+	Expected    string `json:"expected"`
+	ContextCode string `json:"context_code"`
+	CodeForm    string `json:"code_form"`
+	Answer      string `json:"answer"`
+}
+
+// findContentDir walks up from start until it finds the editorial package.
+func findContentDir(start string) (string, error) {
+	dir, err := filepath.Abs(start)
+	if err != nil {
+		return "", err
+	}
+	for {
+		candidate := filepath.Join(dir, filepath.FromSlash(contentDirName))
+		if _, err := os.Stat(filepath.Join(candidate, "exercicios.json")); err == nil {
+			return candidate, nil
+		}
+		parent := filepath.Dir(dir)
+		if parent == dir {
+			return "", fmt.Errorf("content package %s not found above %s; pass -content", contentDirName, start)
+		}
+		dir = parent
+	}
+}
+
+func readJSON(path string, into any) error {
+	raw, err := os.ReadFile(path)
+	if err != nil {
+		return err
+	}
+	if err := json.Unmarshal(raw, into); err != nil {
+		return fmt.Errorf("%s: %w", path, err)
+	}
+	return nil
+}
+
+// loadContent reads curriculo.json and exercicios.json and keeps the concepts up to throughStage.
+// It rejects content the existing engine cannot serve as written, instead of serving part of it.
+func loadContent(dir string, throughStage int) (*content, error) {
+	var curriculum struct {
+		Version string `json:"version"`
+		Stages  []struct {
+			Order          int    `json:"order"`
+			LessonMarkdown string `json:"lesson_markdown"`
+		} `json:"stages"`
+		Concepts []conceptSpec `json:"concepts"`
+	}
+	var exercises struct {
+		Version  string   `json:"version"`
+		Families []family `json:"families"`
+	}
+	if err := readJSON(filepath.Join(dir, "curriculo.json"), &curriculum); err != nil {
+		return nil, err
+	}
+	if err := readJSON(filepath.Join(dir, "exercicios.json"), &exercises); err != nil {
+		return nil, err
+	}
+	if curriculum.Version == "" || curriculum.Version != exercises.Version {
+		return nil, fmt.Errorf("content versions differ: curriculum %q, exercises %q", curriculum.Version, exercises.Version)
+	}
+	c := &content{Version: curriculum.Version, families: map[string]*family{}, lessons: map[int]string{}}
+	for _, s := range curriculum.Stages {
+		c.lessons[s.Order] = s.LessonMarkdown
+	}
+	all := map[string]*family{}
+	for i := range exercises.Families {
+		all[exercises.Families[i].ID] = &exercises.Families[i]
+	}
+	served := map[string]bool{}
+	for _, spec := range curriculum.Concepts {
+		if spec.Stage >= 1 && spec.Stage <= throughStage {
+			c.Concepts = append(c.Concepts, spec)
+			served[spec.ID] = true
+		}
+	}
+	if len(c.Concepts) == 0 {
+		return nil, fmt.Errorf("no concept in stages 1 to %d", throughStage)
+	}
+	for _, spec := range c.Concepts {
+		if strings.TrimSpace(c.lessons[spec.Stage]) == "" {
+			return nil, fmt.Errorf("%s: stage %d has no lesson", spec.ID, spec.Stage)
+		}
+		for _, pre := range spec.Prerequisites {
+			if !served[pre] {
+				return nil, fmt.Errorf("%s: prerequisite %s is outside stages 1 to %d", spec.ID, pre, throughStage)
+			}
+		}
+		if len(spec.FamilyIDs) == 0 || len(spec.FamilyIDs) > maxFamiliesPerConcept {
+			return nil, fmt.Errorf("%s: %d families; the practice route serves 1 to %d sections", spec.ID, len(spec.FamilyIDs), maxFamiliesPerConcept)
+		}
+		questions := map[string]string{}
+		for _, id := range spec.FamilyIDs {
+			f := all[id]
+			if f == nil || f.ConceptID != spec.ID {
+				return nil, fmt.Errorf("%s: family %s is missing or belongs to another concept", spec.ID, id)
+			}
+			if len(f.Cases) != casesPerFamily {
+				return nil, fmt.Errorf("%s: %d cases; the study page rotates a bank of %d", id, len(f.Cases), casesPerFamily)
+			}
+			for _, k := range f.Cases {
+				if strings.TrimSpace(k.Prompt) == "" || strings.TrimSpace(k.Answer) == "" || strings.TrimSpace(k.Explanation) == "" {
+					return nil, fmt.Errorf("%s: prompt, answer and explanation are required", k.ID)
+				}
+				if f.Mode == "choice" && choiceText(k, k.Answer) == "" {
+					return nil, fmt.Errorf("%s: answer %q is not one of its choices", k.ID, k.Answer)
+				}
+				q := questionText(k)
+				if other, dup := questions[q]; dup {
+					return nil, fmt.Errorf("%s and %s serve the same question in %s", other, k.ID, spec.ID)
+				}
+				questions[q] = k.ID
+			}
+			c.families[id] = f
+		}
+	}
+	return c, nil
+}
+
+func choiceText(k exerciseCase, id string) string {
+	for _, option := range k.Choices {
+		if option.ID == id {
+			return option.Text
+		}
+	}
+	return ""
+}
+
+// questionText is the text served to the learner and stored with each attempt and report.
+// Choices use the "A) text" blocks the existing interface already turns into buttons.
+func questionText(k exerciseCase) string {
+	var b strings.Builder
+	b.WriteString(strings.TrimSpace(k.Prompt))
+	if code := strings.TrimSpace(k.ContextCode); code != "" {
+		b.WriteString("\n\n```ts\n" + code + "\n```")
+	}
+	for _, option := range k.Choices {
+		b.WriteString("\n\n" + option.ID + ") " + strings.TrimSpace(option.Text))
+	}
+	return b.String()
+}
+
+// expectedText is the reference answer shown after grading. The engine hands it back to Grade.
+// For a choice it is the text of the correct alternative: the engine resolves the letter a learner
+// taps or types to the text of that alternative in the served question before it grades.
+func expectedText(f *family, k exerciseCase) string {
+	if f.Mode == "choice" {
+		return strings.TrimSpace(choiceText(k, k.Answer))
+	}
+	return k.Answer
+}
+
+func (c *content) familiesOf(spec conceptSpec) []*family {
+	list := make([]*family, 0, len(spec.FamilyIDs))
+	for _, id := range spec.FamilyIDs {
+		list = append(list, c.families[id])
+	}
+	return list
+}
+
+// lookup returns the family and case that serve a question of a concept. A report stores the
+// concept and the question, so this recovers the instance under review without new storage.
+func (c *content) lookup(conceptID, question string) (familyID, caseID string, ok bool) {
+	for _, spec := range c.Concepts {
+		if spec.ID != conceptID {
+			continue
+		}
+		for _, f := range c.familiesOf(spec) {
+			for _, k := range f.Cases {
+				if questionText(k) == question {
+					return f.ID, k.ID, true
+				}
+			}
+		}
+	}
+	return "", "", false
+}
+
+// exercise serves one concept. Each family is one section of the lesson (a knowledge point),
+// so the existing stepper keeps a learner on the same family after a wrong answer.
+type exercise struct {
+	families []*family
+	check    *checker
+	draw     atomic.Int64
+	jobs     map[string]gradeJob
+	hint     string
+}
+
+func newExercise(spec conceptSpec, families []*family, check *checker) (*exercise, error) {
+	g := &exercise{families: families, check: check, jobs: map[string]gradeJob{}, hint: "Selecione uma alternativa ou digite a letra dela."}
+	for _, f := range families {
+		if f.Mode != "choice" {
+			g.hint = "Digite somente a resposta solicitada, sem bloco Markdown."
+		}
+		for _, k := range f.Cases {
+			key := expectedText(f, k)
+			job := gradeJob{Case: k.ID, Family: f.ID, Mode: f.Mode, Expected: key, ContextCode: k.ContextCode, CodeForm: k.CodeForm}
+			// The engine gives Grade only the reference answer. Two cases may share it only when
+			// the grader would receive the same task for both. A choice is judged by its text alone.
+			if other, seen := g.jobs[key]; seen {
+				sameTask := other.Mode == job.Mode && (job.Mode == "choice" || other.Family == job.Family) &&
+					(job.Mode != "code" || other.ContextCode == job.ContextCode)
+				if !sameTask {
+					return nil, fmt.Errorf("%s: cases %s and %s share the reference answer %q with different tasks", spec.ID, other.Case, k.ID, key)
+				}
+				continue
+			}
+			g.jobs[key] = job
+		}
+	}
+	return g, nil
+}
+
+func (g *exercise) Generate(ctx generator.GeneratorContext) generator.Problem {
+	// Mathua calls Generate repeatedly with one context in a batch. The local counter walks the
+	// finite bank, so every case of the section is reached before one repeats.
+	n := uint64(ctx.Seed) + uint64(g.draw.Add(1))
+	var f *family
+	var k exerciseCase
+	if ctx.KPOrdinal >= 1 && ctx.KPOrdinal <= len(g.families) {
+		f = g.families[ctx.KPOrdinal-1]
+		k = f.Cases[n%uint64(len(f.Cases))]
+	} else {
+		total := 0
+		for _, each := range g.families {
+			total += len(each.Cases)
+		}
+		at := int(n % uint64(total))
+		for _, each := range g.families {
+			if at < len(each.Cases) {
+				f, k = each, each.Cases[at]
+				break
+			}
+			at -= len(each.Cases)
+		}
+	}
+	return generator.Problem{Question: questionText(k), Answer: expectedText(f, k), Explanation: k.Explanation}
+}
+
+func (g *exercise) Grade(expected, answer string) grader.Result {
+	job, ok := g.jobs[expected]
+	if !ok {
+		return grader.Result{Unavailable: true, Feedback: "Esta pergunta não pertence à versão atual do conteúdo. Sua resposta não foi contada como erro."}
+	}
+	job.Answer = answer
+	return g.check.grade(job)
+}
+
+func (g *exercise) AnswerFormat() generator.AnswerFormat {
+	return generator.AnswerFormat{InputMode: "text", Hint: g.hint}
+}
+
+// gradingTypes keeps the values the three original pilot concepts were registered with.
+var gradingTypes = map[string]string{"ts.value.numeric_literal": "numeric"}
+
+func (c *content) dag() (*concepts.DAG, error) {
+	list := make([]concepts.Concept, 0, len(c.Concepts))
+	for _, spec := range c.Concepts {
+		gradingType := gradingTypes[spec.ID]
+		if gradingType == "" {
+			gradingType = "expression"
+		}
+		// The prerequisite edges are the package's teaching choice, and the thresholds are the
+		// ones the pilot already used. This integration adds no new statistical threshold.
+		list = append(list, concepts.Concept{ID: spec.ID, Label: spec.Label, Domain: "typescript", GradingType: gradingType,
+			Prerequisites: spec.Prerequisites, MasteryThreshold: concepts.MasteryThreshold{Streak: 3, AvgTimeSeconds: 60}})
+	}
+	return concepts.Build(list)
+}
+
+func sectionLabel(spec conceptSpec, index int) string {
+	if len(spec.FamilyIDs) == 1 {
+		return spec.Label
+	}
+	return fmt.Sprintf("%s (parte %d de %d)", spec.Label, index+1, len(spec.FamilyIDs))
+}
+
+// writeLessons writes the files the existing lesson loader reads. The package has one lesson per
+// stage, so every concept of a stage shows that lesson, once per section.
+func (c *content) writeLessons(dir string) error {
+	if err := os.MkdirAll(filepath.Join(dir, "kp"), 0o755); err != nil {
+		return err
+	}
+	type entry struct {
+		ConceptID string `json:"concept_id"`
+		Source    string `json:"source"`
+	}
+	type point struct {
+		Label    string   `json:"label"`
+		Section  string   `json:"section"`
+		Subgoals []string `json:"subgoals"`
+	}
+	index := make([]entry, 0, len(c.Concepts))
+	for _, spec := range c.Concepts {
+		var md strings.Builder
+		md.WriteString("# " + spec.Label + "\n")
+		points := make([]point, 0, len(spec.FamilyIDs))
+		for i := range spec.FamilyIDs {
+			label := sectionLabel(spec, i)
+			md.WriteString("\n## " + label + "\n\n" + strings.TrimSpace(c.lessons[spec.Stage]) + "\n")
+			points = append(points, point{Label: label, Section: label, Subgoals: []string{}})
+		}
+		if err := os.WriteFile(filepath.Join(dir, spec.ID+".md"), []byte(md.String()), 0o644); err != nil {
+			return err
+		}
+		raw, err := json.Marshal(points)
+		if err != nil {
+			return err
+		}
+		if err := os.WriteFile(filepath.Join(dir, "kp", spec.ID+".json"), raw, 0o644); err != nil {
+			return err
+		}
+		index = append(index, entry{ConceptID: spec.ID, Source: spec.ID + ".md"})
+	}
+	raw, err := json.Marshal(index)
+	if err != nil {
+		return err
+	}
+	return os.WriteFile(filepath.Join(dir, "lessons.json"), raw, 0o644)
+}
+
+// verifyModelAnswers grades every served case with its own model answer in one grader process.
+// A missing grader, a broken context or a model answer its grader rejects stops the start.
+func (c *content) verifyModelAnswers(check *checker) error {
+	var jobs []gradeJob
+	for _, spec := range c.Concepts {
+		for _, f := range c.familiesOf(spec) {
+			for _, k := range f.Cases {
+				reference := expectedText(f, k)
+				jobs = append(jobs, gradeJob{Case: k.ID, Family: f.ID, Mode: f.Mode, Expected: reference,
+					ContextCode: k.ContextCode, CodeForm: k.CodeForm, Answer: reference})
+			}
+		}
+	}
+	results, err := check.gradeBatch(jobs)
+	if err != nil {
+		return fmt.Errorf("content self-check could not run: %w", err)
+	}
+	var failed []string
+	for i, result := range results {
+		if result.Unavailable || !result.Correct {
+			failed = append(failed, fmt.Sprintf("%s (%s)", jobs[i].Case, result.Feedback))
+		}
+	}
+	if len(failed) > 0 {
+		return fmt.Errorf("%d of %d model answers are not accepted by their grader: %s", len(failed), len(jobs), strings.Join(failed, "; "))
+	}
+	return nil
+}
